Add g_runtime_ram_overlay_select: a game-owned selector that picks the
dispatch table of the code image resident in RAM.

Some games copy different, immutable code images to the same RAM addresses
at different times (overlays). gba_recompile --output-prefix can compile each
image as its own corpus ("so multiple immutable code images can be linked into
one executable", tools/gba_recompile/main.cpp:139-141), but nothing in the
runtime looks up a prefixed table without a resumable context: a corpus holds
one function per (address, instruction set), and dispatch_resolve and the
heal tier look a PC up by address and CPSR.T only. With one image declared as
a [[code_copy]], its body runs at that address whichever image is resident:
the wrong code, silently, with no dispatch miss even under
GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC (repro below). The existing mechanisms do not fit:

- g_runtime_ram_dispatch_hook must run the body inside the callback, since
  dispatch_resolve returns nullptr after it (runtime_arm.cpp:882-894). Every
  transfer inside the overlay then goes back through the hook and adds a host
  frame instead of a tail call: thousands nested in a long overlay loop, and a
  stack overflow under WebAssembly. Its documented contract is narrower
  (byte-verified position-independent routines, runtime_arm.h:65-70).
- g_runtime_force_interp_hook interprets the overlay: never strict-static,
  and a predicate call before every generated instruction.
- RAM heals are keyed by PC and mode only (heal_key, overlay_loader.cpp:35)
  and never invalidated, so a heal built from one image runs for the other.

This patch adds:

- RuntimeRamOverlaySelect g_runtime_ram_overlay_select (runtime_arm.h, with
  the contract). dispatch_resolve consults it for RAM PCs
  (0x02000000-0x03FFFFFF), after g_runtime_ram_dispatch_hook and before the
  static table. It returns 0 (not claimed: the static table answers, as
  without a selector), 1 with a generated DispatchEntry table (look the PC up
  there) or -1 (claimed, no compiled image matches). A selected entry is
  returned from dispatch_resolve and entered exactly like a static hit
  (GBARECOMP_TAIL_CALL from runtime_dispatch_tail, resume alias, yield ABI),
  so nothing runs inside the callback and no host frame is added. A claimed
  PC with no entry is an ordinary dispatch miss: recorded, bridged, fatal
  under GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC.
- runtime_has_static_entry consults it the same way, so the bridge's
  heal-to-static check hands control back only to an entry of the resident
  image. That check runs after every instruction a top-level or
  IRQ-continuation bridge interprets (runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp:640-641
  at the pin), so the selector is called once per such instruction at a RAM
  PC, as well as at every dispatch to one: it should be cheap.
- Claimed PCs skip both heal tiers: the overlay_try_dispatch tier in
  dispatch_resolve, and overlay_request_compile in runtime_dispatch_miss
  (through runtime_ram_overlay_claims()), because of the PC-only heal key
  above. The miss is still recorded and reported; it is bridged instead.
- RuntimeArmContext::ram_overlay_select, captured and restored with the other
  hooks (runtime_arm.cpp:1597-1635), so that each guest of a multiplayer
  session has its own selector and the single-machine one survives a session.
  With a resumable context, the session's ram_dispatch_boundary runs before
  the selector reads guest memory, as before the RAM hook, and a claimed miss
  throws like any missing native entry there. The session scheduler's
  batching classification (multiplayer_session.cpp) is unchanged: a RAM
  dispatch inside a native slice yields at that boundary, as nested RAM hook
  dispatches do. No multiplayer session was run with a selector; only the
  unit tests below cover this part.
- run_game() resets it on exit with the other game-owned dispatch hooks.

What the selector checks is up to the game; the engine checks nothing
itself. The selectors this patch was tested with compare the 32-bit word at
the dispatch target with the word at the same offset of each image's ROM
source, and nothing else. The selector runs only at dispatch. gba_recompile
compiles a direct B or BL to any function of the same corpus as a C call by
name (tools/gba_recompile/main.cpp:784-789 builds the name map from the
whole corpus; external/arm-recomp-core/profiles/armv4t_gba/arm_codegen.cpp:
511-546, at arm-recomp-core 1f13cdf, uses it), which is never re-checked:
inside a selected body, but also from other code of the same corpus that
reaches an overlay range, such as EWRAM code (within BL range of IWRAM) or
the tail of a longer image, which stays in IWRAM while a shorter one is
resident. Only branches to unknown targets go through runtime_dispatch. And
a word-by-word copy leaves every word matching one image or the other, so a
dispatch in the middle of a copy would select a corpus rather than miss.
Such a selector is correct only under the game's own invariants: images that
differ at every dispatch target, no code modified after it is copied, no
code running in the overlap while an image is being copied, and no direct
branch (B or BL) into an overlay range from code outside the resident image.

Upstream may prefer its own mechanisms. Its documented answer for bytes that
change under a static entry is g_runtime_force_interp_hook
(runtime_arm.h:72-78). RuntimeArmContext::program_dispatch is meant for
heterogeneous images (runtime_context.h:44-46) but applies only with a
resumable context, and its callback runs the body itself. A per-program
registry that binds dispatch tables and fails closed on unknown PCs is planned
for mixed-cartridge multiplayer (docs/MIXED_CARTRIDGE_MULTIPLAYER.md,
"Implementation order", step 1). The part of this patch that matters is the
resolve shape, returning the entry for the engine to enter, which a
program_dispatch variant usable without a resumable context could take over;
a global was the smallest change for a single-machine game.

Tested on macOS arm64 (Apple clang 21) at gbarecomp 5e89943 plus this series:

- gbarecomp's ctest: 50 of 50 pass (the two gba-tests test ROM tests were
  not run locally). tail_dispatch_tests gains the selector's cases: selected
  entry and resume alias through runtime_dispatch, the tail and the exchange
  dispatchers; a Thumb PC absent from the selected table, -1, and a claim
  without a table are misses with the original target that never reach the
  heal tier; an unclaimed PC falls through to the static table and the heal
  tier; non-RAM PCs never reach the selector; a handled RAM hook comes first
  and a declining one leaves the PC to the selector; runtime_has_static_entry
  and runtime_ram_overlay_claims; context capture and restore; with a
  resumable context, the boundary before the selector and a throw on a
  claimed miss. Each of three mutations of the patch (heal-to-static answers
  1 for any claimed PC, -1 treated as unclaimed, resume PC not set) fails it.
- A synthetic 4 KiB ROM (no Nintendo data, header logo zero; a stand-in BIOS
  or the retail one; --bios-skip-intro, 2,000 frames,
  GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC=1). Once per frame it copies image A (4 words) to
  IWRAM 0x03000000 and calls it, then image B (5 words) and calls it; A
  writes 0xA1 and B 0xB2 to the same EWRAM word, which the main loop logs
  after each call. A is in the main corpus, B in a second one built with
  --output-prefix ovl_, and the selector compares the live word at the
  target with each image's ROM source. With the selector: FULLY_STATIC,
  0 misses, 0xA1 then 0xB2 in all 2,001 per-frame records. Without it (not
  installed, or gbarecomp without this patch): FULLY_STATIC, 0 misses, and
  0xA1 then 0xA1, A's body run for B. The generator, configs
  and selector are tests/repro/ of
  https://github.com/nvergez/moto-racer-advanced-browser (make_rom.py
  overlay1, overlay1.toml, overlay1.ovl.toml, overlay1_hooks.cpp); attach them
  when filing this patch.
- The heal-tier guard in runtime_dispatch_miss (tail_dispatch_tests links a
  stub runtime_dispatch_miss, so the unit tests do not reach it), on the same
  ROM with RAM healing on (GBARECOMP_SELFHEAL_RECOMPILE=1,
  GBARECOMP_RAM_OVERLAY_HEAL=1, gcc backend, 2,000 frames), no corpus for
  either image, and a selector that answers 0 for A and -1 for anything else:
  A's first dispatch heals 0x03000000 from A's bytes. With the guard, B's
  claimed miss is bridged (5 interpreted instructions per cycle, 10,005 in
  all) and logs 0xB2 in all 2,001 records. With the guard removed, the heal
  tier runs A's heal for B, which logs 0xA1 (4,002 heal calls, 0
  interpreted); so does the same run with the selector uninstalled. This is
  the overlay1heal case of the same repository (overlay1heal.toml,
  overlay1heal_hooks.cpp); it needs a C++ compiler at run time.
- Unchanged when no selector is set: a commercial game (Moto Racer Advance),
  booted without input for 3,000 frames with the retail BIOS, gives
  byte-identical per-frame captures (frame CRC, per-line state, memory
  fields, RAM hashes, FIFO and IO counts, clock and bridged instructions at
  every VBlank start) and identical final lines with and without this patch,
  recompiled and with GBARECOMP_FORCE_INTERP=1.

