+// Optional game-owned selector for RAM overlays: different immutable code
+// images that the game copies to the same RAM addresses at different times,
+// each compiled as its own corpus (gba_recompile --output-prefix writes
+// <prefix>kDispatchTable and <prefix>kDispatchTableLen). Consulted for RAM PCs
+// (0x02000000-0x03FFFFFF) after g_runtime_ram_dispatch_hook and before the
+// static table, by runtime_dispatch and by runtime_has_static_entry, so the
+// interpreter bridge also heals back only into the image the selector picks.
+// It returns
+//    0  not claimed: the static table answers, as without a selector;
+//    1  claimed: look (pc, thumb) up in *table, a generated DispatchEntry
+//       array of *len entries as gba_recompile emits it: sorted by address
+//       (the lookup is a binary search) and never freed (lookups cache
+//       pointers into it);
+//   -1  claimed, but no compiled image matches the live bytes.
+// A claimed PC found in *table is entered exactly like a static hit (tail
+// call, resume alias), so long-running overlay code keeps the generated
+// call/return and yield ABI; unlike g_runtime_ram_dispatch_hook, nothing runs
+// inside the callback. A claimed PC with no entry is an ordinary dispatch miss
+// (recorded, bridged, fatal under GBARECOMP_STRICT_STATIC), except that the
+// self-heal tiers are skipped for it: heals are keyed by PC and mode only, so
+// the heal tier would run whichever image's heal was compiled first.
+// The engine checks nothing itself: what identifies the resident image is the
+// selector's choice, made only for transfers that go through the dispatcher.
+// gba_recompile compiles a direct B or BL to any function of the same corpus
+// as a C call by name, which is never re-checked: inside a selected body, but
+// also from other code of that corpus, such as EWRAM code (within BL range of
+// IWRAM) or the tail of a longer image, which stays in IWRAM while a shorter
+// one is resident. Such a branch runs that corpus's body whichever image is
+// resident, so a game that uses a selector must not branch directly into an
+// overlay range from code outside the resident image.
+// The selector is called on every dispatch to a RAM PC, possibly more than
+// once, and, through runtime_has_static_entry, after every instruction that a
+// top-level or IRQ-continuation interpreter bridge runs when the next PC is
+// in RAM, so it should be cheap. It must not change guest or bus state (read
+// guest memory directly, not through bus_read_*). In a multiplayer session it
+// is per-guest state (RuntimeArmContext::ram_overlay_select), called after
+// the session's RAM dispatch boundary, and a claimed miss throws like any
+// missing native entry.
+typedef int (*RuntimeRamOverlaySelect)(uint32_t pc, int thumb,
+                                       const void** table, unsigned* len);
+extern RuntimeRamOverlaySelect g_runtime_ram_overlay_select;
+// Non-zero when g_runtime_ram_overlay_select claims (pc, thumb), whether or not
+// the selected table has an entry for it. runtime_dispatch_miss uses it to
+// keep claimed PCs out of the self-heal tier.
+int runtime_ram_overlay_claims(uint32_t pc, int thumb);
